abstract: "Caputo, Chen, Gu and Polyanskiy (2025) conjectured that one step of the half-lazy random walk on the complete bipartite graph K_(n,n) shrinks relative entropy by at most the factor log n / (2 log 2n), with single vertices as the only worst starts; they proved n = 3 by computer. This package proves the conjecture for every n, and for K_(m,n) with M = max(m,n), by writing one step of the walk as two copies of the reverse-erasure channel (a visible symbol passes through unchanged and a blank, produced by an erasure channel that keeps each symbol with probability alpha, is redrawn from a prior). For that channel the relative-entropy contraction coefficient depends on the prior only through its smallest atom q, at every alpha, and equals log(1/q)/log(1/(alpha q)) up to the sharp threshold alpha = q^(q/(1-q)); the upper bound is a translation of Gao's weighted mean inequality and the uniform binary case is known. Further results: a rank-one weighted extension (settling the lazy-star observation of Jadhav 2026), a reduction of block-reset chains to at most two states per block, and the exact one-way agreement-distillation rate below the threshold. Unrefereed candidate; internal AI-assisted review only. Text, data and figures CC0-1.0; code MIT."
